      "text": "First, we want to factor out the largest number that appears in both 40 and 48; we need the greatest common factor. We can see that the 40 can be factored into the following.\n\\begin{align*}\n40 & = 1 \\cdot 40 \\\\\n& = 2 \\cdot 20 \\\\\n& = 4 \\cdot 10 \\\\\n& = 5 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n& = 2^3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2^3 \\\\\n& = 2^5\n\\end{align*}\nThe biggest number we can factor out from 40 is $2^3=8$. We can see in the second factorization that $48$ can be written as\n\\begin{align*}\n48 & = 1 \\cdot 48 \\\\\n& = 2 \\cdot 24 \\\\\n& = 3 \\cdot 16 \\\\\n& = 4 \\cdot 12 \\\\\n& = 6 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n& = 8 \\cdot 6 \\\\\n& = 2^4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2^2 \\\\\n& = 2^6\n\\end{align*}\nOur greatest common factor is $8$ because $8$ is the largest number that both 40 and 48 can be divided into evenly.",
